// @run
// @log 2
// @log 1.5
// @log 63
// @hint redundant type annotation: `peekF32` is inferred as `f32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `peekByte` is inferred as `i32`
// WAS AN `@emit-error` FENCE — inverted by D-CAPCALLEE, which is the S1 fix
// `docs/internals/buffer-design.md` §B3/O7 asked for.
//
// A load width called inside a named function, in a module that ALSO uses a
// function value (the `.map` lambda below). On master this failed with
// `captured variable not found in enclosing frame`: capture analysis recorded
// every free identifier that is not locally bound, a module global or a
// collected function, and a memory intrinsic is none of those — so its NAME
// rode the capture list and resolution found nothing to read it from. Censused
// on master over all eleven memory intrinsics x four call positions: 22 of 44
// failed, uniformly the two positions "inside a lambda body" and "named
// function in a module using a function value".
//
// The fix is POSITIONAL, not another entry on a name list: `capScan`'s `Call`
// arm skips a builtin/intrinsic in CALLEE position, where it denotes
// instructions the emitter lowers inline rather than a value. Its control
// `load-width-in-named-fn.vl` (the same module with no lambda) ran on master
// and still runs, so the single variable between them is capture analysis.
//
// 0x3FC00000 (f32 1.5) at address 0 is the bytes 00 00 C0 3F, so the byte at
// offset 3 is 0x3F = 63 — the top byte of the float, read through the same
// memory the f32 load reads.
function peekF32(a: i32): f32 { __load_f32__(a) }
function peekByte(a: i32): i32 { __load_u8__(a) }

__store_i32__(0, 0x3FC00000)
const xs = [1, 2, 3]
const doubled = xs.map((v: i32) => v * 2)
print(doubled[0])
print(peekF32(0))
print(peekByte(3))
